Make TRE's assert() abort instead of raising an R error.

R's copy of TRE maps assert() to Rf_error() (src/extra/tre/tre-internal.h),
so that a broken invariant in the regex engine raises "assertion '...'
failed in executing regexp" instead of ending R.  The harnesses catch and
discard an R error like any rejected input, so a reachable assertion --
a bug in TRE, which a stock build, where configure defines NDEBUG, runs
straight past -- would never be reported.  abort() instead: the
sanitizer runtime prints where it happened and ClusterFuzz files it under
that location.

Of the targets, only agrep drives TRE with fuzzed patterns, and it is
deferred from OSS-Fuzz (DEFERRED_TARGETS in ossfuzz.sh), so this matters
for ClusterFuzzLite runs and local fuzzing.  Assumes patches/0006, 0072
and 0073, which fix the assertions known to be reachable; without them
the agrep target aborts on stored inputs.

Instrumentation, not a fix.  Not for upstream.

diff --git a/src/extra/tre/tre-internal.h b/src/extra/tre/tre-internal.h
index 7db0ebb489..4dcd661a15 100644
--- a/src/extra/tre/tre-internal.h
+++ b/src/extra/tre/tre-internal.h
@@ -37,7 +37,12 @@ extern void Rf_error(const char *str, ...);
 #ifdef NDEBUG
 #define R_assert(e) ((void) 0)
 #else
-#define R_assert(e) ((e) ? (void) 0 : Rf_error("assertion '%s' failed in executing regexp: file '%s', line %d\n", #e, __FILE__, __LINE__))
+/* The harnesses catch and discard an R error like any rejected input, so a
+   failed TRE invariant would never be reported.  Abort instead: the
+   sanitizer prints where it failed and the report deduplicates on it. */
+#include <stdio.h>
+#include <stdlib.h>
+#define R_assert(e) ((e) ? (void) 0 : (fprintf(stderr, "assertion '%s' failed in executing regexp: file '%s', line %d\n", #e, __FILE__, __LINE__), abort()))
 #endif /* NDEBUG */
 
 #undef assert
